// The C64 redraws a 3x3 reel in about a frame (an assembly composer for 32x32 symbols: 48 glyphs,
// 384 bytes a redraw), so far from its stop a reel hops a whole symbol (32 pixels) a redraw: a
// blur, which is what a fast reel looks like. Within four symbols it steps 8 pixels, within one
// symbol 4, and the last 8 pixels 2 (reelscroll.8bs). The hop has to divide a symbol's 32 pixels or
// the reel would not land on its stop. test/glyph-budget.test.mjs checks REEL_FAR and EASE against
// the tile data, so changing the art's size without changing them fails a test.
// WAIT and FAR are the 5x5's (its glyph reels ignore FAR and hop 8: src/labs/slot5x5/spin.8bs).
//
// REEL_WAIT is 0: every reel that is spinning steps on every pass of the game loop. The 3x3 draws its reels
// into a second copy of the character set and shows them all at once (glyphs.8bs, "Double buffering"), so a
// reel that sits out a pass costs a copy of its glyphs into the hidden set (about 0.3 of a frame); with all the
// reels stepping together none does, and each reel steps about as often as before — a pass is three redraws
// long, and a redraw is most of a frame. The reels still start one pass apart.
export namespace feel {
    const WAIT: utinyint = 1;
    const FAR: utinyint = 12;
    const REEL_WAIT: utinyint = 0;
    const REEL_FAR: utinyint = 32;
    const EASE: utinyint = 32; // a symbol (4 cells) here
}
